// Pathological witness, the SILENT half: an argument written at the CAPTURED
// type must be refused.
//
// READ THE TYPES HERE AS DELIBERATELY WRONG. This file is a refutation golden.
// Every type it writes is the type a CAPTURING instantiation produces, not the
// type the signature owes. A reader who takes these as a model of correct
// gandr has read the file backwards.
//
// WHY A SECOND FILE AND NOT A SECOND DEFINITION. Capture makes two types that
// should differ coincide as readily as it makes two that should agree diverge.
// The loud half of the fault is a refusal, and a repair verified only against
// the refused program proves capture stopped rejecting something correct while
// saying nothing about what it was accepting. The two halves are opposite
// outcomes -- one file must run clean, this one must not -- so they cannot be
// stated in one file, and this one carries the assertion whose failure mode is
// ACCEPTANCE.
//
// THE ARITHMETIC THIS PINS. Instantiating comp at (a, c, d) replaces its `b`
// by the caller's `c`, which comp itself binds. A capturing engine substitutes
// one parameter at a time and lets that binder swallow the replacement, so the
// expectation for comp's second function degrades from U[ω] (c -> F d) to
// U[ω] (d -> F d), and the result degrades from U[ω] (a -> F d) to
// U[ω] (a -> F c). Both degradations are written out below, which is exactly
// why a capturing engine ACCEPTS this file: every type here agrees with what
// capture produces. An engine that renames the binder apart refuses it,
// because `h` is then supplied at U[ω] (d -> F d) where U[ω] (c -> F d) is
// owed.
//
// SO THE GREEN AND THE RED SAY OPPOSITE THINGS FROM THE USUAL DIRECTION. This
// file passing means the engine REFUSED the program. This file failing means
// the engine ACCEPTED it, which is the silent fault firing.

//@ expect-diagnostic: type mismatch: expected Value(Thunk(Omega, Arrow(Atom("c"), F(Atom("d"))))), actual Value(Thunk(Omega, Arrow(Atom("d"), F(Atom("d")))))

def comp(a : Type, b : Type, c : Type, f : U[ω] (a -> F b), g : U[ω] (b -> F c), x : a) -> F c {
  run y <- f(x);
  g(y)
}

// `h` is supplied at the CAPTURED spelling U[ω] (d -> F d), and the result is
// ascribed at the CAPTURED spelling U[ω] (a -> F c). Both are what capture
// produces and neither is what the signature owes.
def captured(a : Type, b : Type, c : Type, d : Type, u : U[ω] (a -> F c), h : U[ω] (d -> F d))
  -> F (U[ω] (a -> F c))
{
  ret thunk { comp(a, c, d, u, h) }
}
